1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,470
Welcome back to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:07,299
This is chapter seven: the Stream API.

3
00:00:07,749 --> 00:00:11,949
Streams are the most used, and most misused, part of Java 8.

4
00:00:11,949 --> 00:00:15,660
The API is easy to learn, but the mental model isn't.

5
00:00:15,660 --> 00:00:23,081
Get it wrong and you write pipelines that are slower than the loop they replaced, or that nobody can read.

6
00:00:23,081 --> 00:00:25,391
Let's fix the mental model first.

7
00:00:26,291 --> 00:00:31,617
Here's the task: total the value of perishable cart lines, grouped by vendor.

8
00:00:31,617 --> 00:00:37,151
In Java 7, that's a loop with a continue, a map lookup, a null check, and a put.

9
00:00:37,151 --> 00:00:41,439
Only two ideas matter, filter to perishable and sum by vendor.

10
00:00:41,439 --> 00:00:46,558
Everything else is mechanics, and every line is a place to make a mistake.

11
00:00:47,008 --> 00:00:54,116
The stream version states those two ideas directly: filter, then group by vendor and add up the totals.

12
00:00:54,116 --> 00:00:55,910
The win isn't fewer lines.

13
00:00:55,910 --> 00:00:59,360
It's that the what is visible and the how is gone.

14
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:02,207
A stream is not a collection.

15
00:01:02,207 --> 00:01:04,087
It's a pipeline description.

16
00:01:04,087 --> 00:01:08,921
It starts with a source: a collection, an array, a generator, or a file.

17
00:01:09,371 --> 00:01:13,789
Then come intermediate operations, like filter, map and sorted.

18
00:01:13,789 --> 00:01:14,841
These are lazy.

19
00:01:14,841 --> 00:01:17,715
Calling them just builds up the pipeline.

20
00:01:17,715 --> 00:01:18,907
Nothing runs yet.

21
00:01:19,357 --> 00:01:27,165
Nothing happens until a terminal operation, like collect, reduce or forEach, pulls elements through the whole pipeline.

22
00:01:27,615 --> 00:01:31,071
So compared with a collection, a stream stores nothing.

23
00:01:31,521 --> 00:01:32,828
It can't be reused.

24
00:01:32,828 --> 00:01:37,644
Call a second terminal operation and you get an IllegalStateException.

25
00:01:38,094 --> 00:01:40,846
It's evaluated lazily, not eagerly.

26
00:01:41,296 --> 00:01:45,563
And it can even be infinite, as long as something downstream stops it.

27
00:01:46,463 --> 00:01:54,573
The most important idea in this chapter: elements flow through the whole pipeline one at a time, not stage by stage.

28
00:01:54,573 --> 00:02:01,076
Here we print what filter sees and what map sees, then ask for the first SKU starting with A.

29
00:02:01,526 --> 00:02:02,800
Look at the output.

30
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,416
Filter sees milk, then rice, then atta.

31
00:02:05,416 --> 00:02:08,166
Atta passes, map sees it, and we're done.

32
00:02:08,166 --> 00:02:09,106
Two surprises.

33
00:02:09,106 --> 00:02:13,331
Dal is never examined at all, because findFirst short-circuits.

34
00:02:13,331 --> 00:02:18,228
And map runs on the third element before filter has even seen the fourth.

35
00:02:18,228 --> 00:02:23,393
If you picture filter everything, then map everything, you'd predict neither.

36
00:02:24,293 --> 00:02:25,947
A quick tour of the API.

37
00:02:25,947 --> 00:02:28,565
Filter, map and flatMap are stateless.

38
00:02:28,565 --> 00:02:30,977
Each element is handled on its own.

39
00:02:30,977 --> 00:02:37,041
Distinct, sorted and skip are stateful, because they have to remember what they've seen.

40
00:02:37,041 --> 00:02:41,589
Limit and takeWhile short-circuit, and peek is for debugging only.

41
00:02:41,589 --> 00:02:42,554
We'll see why.

42
00:02:43,004 --> 00:02:50,201
On the terminal side, collect and Java 16's toList build collections, and toList is unmodifiable.

43
00:02:50,201 --> 00:02:52,947
Reduce and count give a single value.

44
00:02:52,947 --> 00:02:58,660
Min, max and findFirst return an Optional, because the stream might be empty.

45
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:03,335
And for numbers, mapToInt then sum avoids boxing every element.

46
00:03:03,785 --> 00:03:07,820
One thing to remember: stateful operations break laziness.

47
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:13,107
Sorted has to consume the entire stream before it can emit a single element.

48
00:03:14,007 --> 00:03:15,328
Map versus flatMap.

49
00:03:15,328 --> 00:03:16,927
Map is one in, one out.

50
00:03:16,927 --> 00:03:23,530
Mapping each order to its lines gives you a stream of lists, which usually isn't what you want.

51
00:03:23,530 --> 00:03:29,508
FlatMap takes a function that returns a stream, and merges all those streams into one.

52
00:03:29,508 --> 00:03:34,999
Now you have a flat stream of order lines you can map to SKUs and de-duplicate.

53
00:03:35,449 --> 00:03:37,886
The same trick flattens Optionals.

54
00:03:37,886 --> 00:03:45,771
Look up each SKU in the catalogue to get a stream of Optionals, then flatMap with Optional colon colon stream.

55
00:03:45,771 --> 00:03:47,993
Empty results simply disappear.

56
00:03:48,893 --> 00:03:53,267
Infinite streams are safe as long as something short-circuits.

57
00:03:53,267 --> 00:03:57,641
Stream dot generate with a limit of five gives five order IDs.

58
00:03:57,641 --> 00:04:05,683
Java 9's three-argument iterate takes a seed, a condition and a step, so the stream ends when the condition fails.

59
00:04:06,133 --> 00:04:10,768
TakeWhile is like filter, but it stops at the first element that fails.

60
00:04:10,768 --> 00:04:18,276
On a list sorted newest first, filter checks every order, while takeWhile stops at the first order from last month.

61
00:04:18,276 --> 00:04:19,125
Much cheaper.

62
00:04:19,575 --> 00:04:22,146
But only because the list is sorted.

63
00:04:22,146 --> 00:04:26,860
On an unsorted stream, takeWhile silently drops every later match.

64
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:29,431
That's a bug no compiler will catch.

65
00:04:30,331 --> 00:04:32,909
Two bugs that tests rarely catch.

66
00:04:32,909 --> 00:04:34,002
First, reduce.

67
00:04:34,002 --> 00:04:40,877
The identity value must really be an identity: combining it with any x must give back x.

68
00:04:40,877 --> 00:04:42,674
Zero with plus is fine.

69
00:04:42,674 --> 00:04:46,502
But zero with times returns zero for every input.

70
00:04:46,502 --> 00:04:47,205
Silently.

71
00:04:47,655 --> 00:04:49,669
Second, Collectors dot toMap.

72
00:04:49,669 --> 00:04:53,488
A cart can legitimately have the same SKU on two lines.

73
00:04:53,488 --> 00:05:01,958
The two-argument toMap throws an IllegalStateException when that happens, at runtime, on data that's rarely in your tests.

74
00:05:01,958 --> 00:05:09,318
Always pass a merge function, here adding the quantities together, unless your keys are guaranteed unique.

75
00:05:09,318 --> 00:05:14,109
toMap also throws on null values, which a plain HashMap would accept.

76
00:05:15,009 --> 00:05:18,855
Under the hood, every stream is driven by a Spliterator.

77
00:05:18,855 --> 00:05:23,870
It traverses elements, and it can split itself in half for parallel work.

78
00:05:23,870 --> 00:05:30,533
It also reports characteristics, like sized, ordered and sorted, that let the pipeline skip work.

79
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:34,379
If the source is already sorted, sorted becomes a no-op.

80
00:05:34,829 --> 00:05:38,539
Intermediate operations are fused into a single pass.

81
00:05:38,539 --> 00:05:43,508
Each element is pushed through every stage before the next one is read.

82
00:05:43,508 --> 00:05:46,448
So a three-stage stream isn't three loops.

83
00:05:46,448 --> 00:05:51,277
It's one loop with three function calls, which the JIT often inlines.

84
00:05:51,727 --> 00:05:54,210
And that's why peek is unreliable.

85
00:05:54,210 --> 00:05:57,641
The JDK may skip work it can prove unnecessary.

86
00:05:57,641 --> 00:06:04,795
Since Java 9, count can answer straight from the source's size, so a peek before it may never run.

87
00:06:04,795 --> 00:06:07,424
Use peek to observe while debugging.

88
00:06:07,424 --> 00:06:09,541
Never use it to do real work.

89
00:06:10,441 --> 00:06:19,137
Parallel streams run on the common ForkJoinPool, which has one thread fewer than your CPU cores and is shared by the whole JVM.

90
00:06:19,137 --> 00:06:34,270
Parallelism helps when the data is large, think ten thousand elements or more, when the work per element is real CPU work, when the source splits cheaply, like an array or ArrayList, and when the operations are stateless.

91
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:40,171
It hurts when the work blocks on I/O, because the small pool just sits waiting.

92
00:06:40,171 --> 00:06:43,206
Use virtual threads or an executor for that.

93
00:06:43,206 --> 00:06:55,349
It hurts on small streams, on sources like LinkedList that split badly, and especially inside a server, where every request's parallel stream competes for that one shared pool.

94
00:06:55,799 --> 00:06:58,057
So the default answer is sequential.

95
00:06:58,057 --> 00:07:02,007
Add parallel when you've measured, not when the data looks big.

96
00:07:02,907 --> 00:07:04,095
Production rules.

97
00:07:04,095 --> 00:07:10,522
Use streams where the loop was just ceremony around a filter, a transform or an aggregation.

98
00:07:10,522 --> 00:07:13,735
Prefer toList, but remember it's unmodifiable.

99
00:07:13,735 --> 00:07:16,110
Use primitive streams for numbers.

100
00:07:16,110 --> 00:07:18,555
Always give toMap a merge function.

101
00:07:18,555 --> 00:07:25,051
Close file streams like Files dot lines with try-with-resources, or you'll leak file handles.

102
00:07:25,051 --> 00:07:28,614
And extract long pipelines into well-named methods.

103
00:07:29,064 --> 00:07:30,457
Never reuse a stream.

104
00:07:30,457 --> 00:07:32,579
Never use peek for side effects.

105
00:07:32,579 --> 00:07:35,629
Never mutate a shared collection from forEach.

106
00:07:35,629 --> 00:07:39,276
Use collect instead, which is correct even in parallel.

107
00:07:39,276 --> 00:07:41,730
Never block inside a parallel stream.

108
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:45,244
Never use an infinite source without a short-circuit.

109
00:07:45,244 --> 00:07:49,820
And if a pipeline is more than about five operations, give it a name.

110
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:53,533
If you can't read it in one breath, the loop was better.

111
00:07:54,433 --> 00:07:59,596
Java 8 delivered streams, primitive streams, collectors and parallel streams.

112
00:08:00,046 --> 00:08:06,008
Java 9 added takeWhile, dropWhile, the bounded iterate, and Optional dot stream.

113
00:08:06,008 --> 00:08:11,225
It also let count skip traversal, which is what makes peek unreliable.

114
00:08:11,675 --> 00:08:19,995
Java 10 added unmodifiable collectors, and Java 12 added teeing, which runs two collectors in a single pass.

115
00:08:20,445 --> 00:08:23,947
Java 16 brought Stream dot toList and mapMulti.

116
00:08:23,947 --> 00:08:31,922
When migrating, check that nobody mutates the list, because the old collector returned a mutable ArrayList.

117
00:08:32,372 --> 00:08:38,601
Java 21's virtual threads gave blocking work a proper home, off parallel streams.

118
00:08:39,051 --> 00:08:48,132
And Java 24 finalised stream gatherers, so you can write your own intermediate operations, like windowing and running totals.

119
00:08:48,132 --> 00:08:50,166
That's chapter thirty-eight.

120
00:08:51,066 --> 00:08:51,678
To recap.

121
00:08:51,678 --> 00:08:59,160
A stream is a lazy pipeline: a source, intermediate operations, and a terminal operation that runs everything.

122
00:08:59,160 --> 00:09:04,601
Elements flow through one at a time, and short-circuiting operations stop early.

123
00:09:04,601 --> 00:09:09,498
FlatMap merges nested streams, and takeWhile only works on sorted input.

124
00:09:09,498 --> 00:09:14,668
Give toMap a merge function, and check that reduce's identity really is one.

125
00:09:14,668 --> 00:09:18,885
And stay sequential until you've measured that parallel helps.

126
00:09:19,335 --> 00:09:26,234
Next is chapter eight, collectors and stream composition, where most of the real work in collect happens.

127
00:09:26,234 --> 00:09:27,548
Thanks for watching.
